<Learn
  title="India Atlas — Learn · Stocks and flows"
  description="Learn № 17 — a stock is an amount at a point in time (wealth, debt, capital); a flow is a rate over a period (income, GDP, deficit, investment). Half of all economic confusion is one mistaken for the other."
  slug="stocks-and-flows"
  kicker="Learn № 17 — Reading numbers · Stocks and flows"
  h1="Wealth is a stock. Income is <i>a flow.</i>"
  lede="One distinction quietly organises half of economics: amounts at a moment versus rates over a period. Debt and deficit, capital and investment, savings and saving. Get the pair straight and a third of the news starts reading differently."
  meta={[
    ["Concept", "Stocks and flows"],
    ["Illustrated with", "Worked examples"],
    ["Source", "MoSPI, NSO · Union Budget"],
    ["Level", "First principles"],
    ["Reading time", "6 min"],
  ]}
  dateStamp="Evergreen"
  crumbTitle="Stocks and flows"
>

  <!-- § 01 amounts and rates -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>Amounts and rates</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">First principles</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>Half of all economic confusion is a stock mistaken for a flow. A stock is an amount at a point in time: the money in your account on 31 March, the gold in a vault, a country's debt, its factories and roads. A flow is a rate over a period: a salary per month, GDP per quarter, a deficit per year. The two carry different units, and swapping them produces sentences that sound sensible and are not.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Stock <span class="sub">an amount · measured at a point in time</span></div>
          <p>A quantity that exists at a moment: wealth, debt, capital, the water in a tank. It has no per-year attached. India's GDP is not a stock; the country's accumulated assets are.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> how much there is, right now.</div>
        </aside>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Flow <span class="sub">a rate · measured over a period</span></div>
          <p>A quantity that only makes sense with time attached: income per month, GDP per quarter, investment per year, a deficit per year. Double the period and you roughly double the flow. A stock does not work that way.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> how much is being added or spent, per unit of time.</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">Picture a bathtub. The water in it is a stock; the tap and the drain are flows. Saving raises the level of wealth; a deficit raises the level of debt. Every stock in economics is the accumulated residue of flows past, and every flow is a stock in the act of changing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · i</span>
          <b>Check the unit.</b> Rupees is a stock. Rupees per year is a flow. If a sentence swaps one for the other, stop reading it.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · ii</span>
          <b>Flows fill and drain.</b> Every stock is what past flows left behind. There is no other way for a stock to change.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · iii</span>
          <b>Rich is a stock word.</b> GDP says how much was produced, not how much is owned. The two questions have different answers.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 the twins -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>The confusable twins</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Four pairs · one plain line each</span></div>
    <div class="break ledger">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>The pair</th><th>Stock / flow</th><th>One plain line</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="name">Wealth / Income<small>having vs earning</small></td>
            <td class="chg">stock / flow</td>
            <td class="name">Wealth is what you have; income is what you earn. A wealthy person can have a bad year.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="name">Debt / Deficit<small>the pile vs the year's addition</small></td>
            <td class="chg">stock / flow</td>
            <td class="name">The deficit is one year's overspend; the debt is every year's overspend added up.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="name">Capital / Investment<small>the machines vs the building of them</small></td>
            <td class="chg">stock / flow</td>
            <td class="name">Capital is the stock of machines, buildings and roads; investment is the year's addition to it.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="name">Savings / Saving<small>the balance vs the habit</small></td>
            <td class="chg">stock / flow</td>
            <td class="name">Savings are the pile accumulated over years; saving is what one year added to it.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 why the mix-up matters -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>Why the mix-up matters</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Three confusions, cleared</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>Debt versus deficit is the classic. A government that has halved its deficit has still increased its debt: more slowly, but increased it, because every deficit adds to the pile. Only a surplus, a negative flow, brings the stock down. The headline sounds like repayment. The arithmetic is a smaller addition.</p>
        <p>GDP versus wealth is the national version. GDP tells you how much the country produced in a year, not how rich the country is. A country can run a high GDP and own little, or sit on old wealth and produce little. A flood can destroy wealth and raise GDP in the same year, because rebuilding is a flow while the loss was a stock. Rich is a stock word; productive is a flow word. They often travel together, and they are not the same.</p>
        <p>Investment versus capital trips the markets pages. High investment in a year means the capital stock is growing quickly, not that it is large. A young economy can out-invest an old one for decades and still hold less capital per person. The flow is the speed. The stock is the distance covered.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · iv</span>
          <b>Every deficit is an addition.</b> Halving the deficit still grows the debt. Only a surplus shrinks it.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · v</span>
          <b>Speed is not distance.</b> High investment is a fast tap, not a full tub. The capital stock is decades of flows minus decades of wear.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 misreads -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>How to misread this concept</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“The deficit is the debt.”</h3>
          <p>No. The deficit is one year's shortfall; the debt is the total of every shortfall still unpaid. A government can shrink the deficit every single year and watch the debt grow every single year. Both things happen at once, regularly.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“GDP tells you how rich a country is.”</h3>
          <p>It tells you how much the country produced in a year. Rich is about accumulated assets: factories, roads, homes, savings. GDP is the tap, not the tub, and a country is a balance sheet as well as a payslip.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“High investment means high wealth.”</h3>
          <p>No: high investment is a fast rate of addition, not a large total. The capital stock is decades of investment minus decades of wear. One year's flow is a small entry against a large inheritance, whoever you are.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div slot="prov-source">
    <h6>Source of record</h6>
    <p>This note is conceptual and cites no series. The pairs it uses correspond to published aggregates: the Union government's deficit and debt in the budget documents, GDP and investment in the national accounts from the Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation, household savings in the Reserve Bank of India's flow-of-funds statistics.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-revision">
    <h6>Definitions &amp; honesty</h6>
    <p>Stock and flow are used in their accounting senses: an amount at a point in time, and a rate over a period. Saving is the flow; savings are the accumulated stock. The bathtub appears once, as licensed by the house style, and then retires.</p>
    <p>No figures are quoted on this page, because the distinction being taught does not depend on any particular year's data.</p>
  </div>
